00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:11.700
COLLEGE IS A SCAM:
We often hear people call college a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.080
scam. When they say this, they&nbsp;
usually mean two things. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.480
they think that college is too expensive or two,&nbsp;
they don’t think that college teaches enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.540
relevant skills that are actually applicable to&nbsp;
a job or career. But, there’s this one college&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.520
called the University of Phoenix that’s not&nbsp;
just a scam figuratively or metaphorically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:40.620
but literally. Now, for profit diploma mills are&nbsp;
nothing new, but usually, their scope and scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:46.260
are relatively small. And the people who join&nbsp;
these institutions are generally aware of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.300
shady status of the school. The University&nbsp;
of Phoenix, however, wasn’t a shell company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.900
located in American Samoa. Instead, they actually&nbsp;
have hundreds of massive locations across the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.540
including some of the most popular cities&nbsp;
like Atlanta, GA; Austin, TX; Las Vegas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:10.740
NV; and of course Phoenix, Arizona. They even&nbsp;
have their own massive stadium. Seeing all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.280
you would think that the institution is very much&nbsp;
legitimate, and technically they are. At the peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:23.100
the university straight up had nearly 500,000&nbsp;
students. And the university also had plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.960
distinguished professors to match. Basically&nbsp;
everything about the institution checks out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:34.440
except for the fact that they’re openly for&nbsp;
profit. Now, if we’re being honest, basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.320
every institution is for profit whether they&nbsp;
like to acknowledge it or not. If they weren’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:47.160
UT wouldn’t have a $42.3 billion endowment and&nbsp;
Harvard wouldn’t have a $50.9 billion endowment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.680
Also, they wouldn’t be paying football coaches&nbsp;
magnitudes more than even their best professors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:59.220
but that’s for wholenother video. The point is&nbsp;
that while traditional institutions are also very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:03.960
much motivated by profits, there’s certain lines&nbsp;
that they won’t cross simply to protect their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:10.200
reputation. Given that the University of Phoenix&nbsp;
is openly for profit though, they don’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:16.680
have a line. In fact, just recently, the FTC fined&nbsp;
the university $50 million for their deceptive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:23.340
practices which was disbursed to 147,500 of their&nbsp;
students. That was in addition to the university&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.880
cancelling $141 million worth of student debt.&nbsp;
So, here’s the story of the world’s most prolific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.040
for profit institution and the various shady&nbsp;
practices that they have leveraged over the years.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.800
HUMBLE ORIGINS:
Usually, when we take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.660
look at the history of shady organizations, we’ll&nbsp;
see that they actually had wholesome beginnings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.060
and the University of Phoenix was no different.&nbsp;
The university was established by a man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:56.640
John Sperling who had a pretty humble background.&nbsp;
John grew up in a tiny house in Missouri and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.520
parents were farmers who couldn’t make ends meet.&nbsp;
So, there was really no expectation for John to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:07.920
go to college himself. In fact, no one in his&nbsp;
entire family had gone to college. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.320
the cards would fall in a way in which John would&nbsp;
not only get a bachelors but a PhD in history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:18.480
from Berkeley. I don’t think you’d be surprised&nbsp;
to hear that he became a professor at San Jose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.480
State University shortly after. By all accounts,&nbsp;
John had made it big. He had gone from being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.000
no name farmer in Missouri to being a respected&nbsp;
professor in California. But, John didn’t fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.100
in all that well with his new crowd. He hated&nbsp;
many of his students as he felt that they didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:40.320
realize how privileged they were. Similarly, he&nbsp;
hated many of his peers as he felt that they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.540
become too comfortable and that they didn’t try&nbsp;
to incite change despite having the power to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:52.800
so. Let’s just say that the university hated John&nbsp;
as well given that he was always a trouble maker.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:58.380
John was literally the president of the faculty&nbsp;
union and he was constantly trying to defy the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:03.000
administration. Hearing all this, it’s surprising&nbsp;
the hear that John stuck around till his 50s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.120
but he eventually hit his breaking point after&nbsp;
a rejection from state administrators. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:14.400
John had been teaching a class for local teachers&nbsp;
and police officers, and what he realized through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.200
this class was that there were plenty of working&nbsp;
adults who wanted higher level degrees. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.120
John wanted to design a program that targetted&nbsp;
working adults, but the state administrators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.920
weren’t exactly big fans of this idea.&nbsp;
First of all, the idea was coming from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:34.320
John who was a known trouble maker. And two,&nbsp;
the school already had plenty of students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.040
so they didn’t really care about attracting&nbsp;
more and they would reject the idea. John,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:43.200
however, felt that this was an example of&nbsp;
the institution exercising classism. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.340
he would leave his cushy job and start pitching&nbsp;
the idea to other universities that were in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:53.220
desperate need of new students, and unlike San&nbsp;
Jose State, these institutions would bite. John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.420
would go onto launch various programs at these&nbsp;
institutions that would eventually become known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:04.560
as IPD. But accreditors weren’t exactly big fans&nbsp;
of this idea. You see, IPD programs were designed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:10.860
to be for profit and had massive margins. But,&nbsp;
wait a minute, didn’t John want to help lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:17.040
class adults? Well, yes, but the only way that&nbsp;
he could get these institutions to not only adopt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.740
this program but to actively care about and grow&nbsp;
it was to offer profit incentive. Despite this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:28.740
the push back from accreditors consinstly became&nbsp;
stronger and stronger, and eventually John would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:32.940
be pushed the conclusion that the only way&nbsp;
he could make this work was by starting his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.380
own institution. He didn’t want to do it in&nbsp;
California where the accreditors already hated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:44.220
him though. So, instead, he moved to Arizona and&nbsp;
established the University of Phoenix in 1976.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.360
CORRUPTED BY PROFIT:&nbsp;

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:54.540
The University was by no means a success from day&nbsp;
one; in fact, it was very much a failure. During&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:00.000
their first year of classes, the institution&nbsp;
only had a total of 8 students. A lot of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.380
had to do with that fact that the university was&nbsp;
unaccredited and had tight stipulations on who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.900
could apply. Some prerequisites stipulated that&nbsp;
you had to be at least 23 years old and already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:15.360
have some work and college experience. But, as the&nbsp;
institution gained accreditation and expanded into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:21.000
more populated areas, they saw greater success&nbsp;
and would eventually become profitable. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.600
their biggest jump forward didn’t come till 1989&nbsp;
when John began offering online programs. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.480
made the university a pioneer in online education&nbsp;
and if you know anything about the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:38.280
you know that investors very much had&nbsp;
a sweet tooth for online products. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:42.660
when the university’s parent company,&nbsp;
Apollo Education Group, went public in 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:48.000
investors ate it up. Investor capital would&nbsp;
allow the institution to grow faster than ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:53.940
before both physically and virtually. Their&nbsp;
programs included business, education, health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:59.580
science, and social science. And they offered&nbsp;
all sorts of degree programs from associates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.620
and bachelors to masters and doctorates. The&nbsp;
university would even expand internationally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.780
by opening a campus in Vancouver in 1998. All&nbsp;
of this allowed the university to grow their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:14.460
enrollment to a total of 100,000 students&nbsp;
by the end of the century. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:20.100
even the largest public institutions in the&nbsp;
US aren’t able to reach such numbers. But, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:26.040
wasn't sunshines and rainbows forever. Investors&nbsp;
don’t invest in companies just for the fun of it;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:33.300
they invest to make profits. And investors don’t&nbsp;
just want profits, they want growing profits which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.220
was especially true in the early 2000s throughout&nbsp;
the dotcom crash. And given that the University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:43.440
of Phoenix was indeed a public company, one of&nbsp;
their legal obligations was to address shareholder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.660
interests. And this is what would set off the&nbsp;
painful decline of the University of Phoenix.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:52.800
SHADY PRACTICES:&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:57.840
The University didn’t just jump into the super&nbsp;
shady stuff straight up though. Like most things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:03.060
their descent into the darkness was much more&nbsp;
gradual starting with much smaller infractions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:09.000
For example, in 2000, they started to drift away&nbsp;
from instructional hours. As you would guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.120
paying a professor to teach a class is obviously&nbsp;
not cheap, so the university started to sprinkle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.520
in some group study hours that would be counted&nbsp;
as instructional hours. The university would end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:26.100
up being fined $6 million for this practice, but&nbsp;
this was just the beginning. A few years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:31.260
two university recruiters would file a lawsuit&nbsp;
against the institution for allegedly predatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.180
recruiting processes. Allegedly, the university&nbsp;
had been paying admission counselors based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:40.680
the number of students that they enrolled which is&nbsp;
actually a violation of the Higher Education Act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.660
For obvious reasons, it’s not a good situation&nbsp;
when admissions counselors are incentivized to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:50.520
act for their own benefit instead of the benefit&nbsp;
of the potential students. The University never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:57.780
admitted to any wrongdoing, but they did agree to&nbsp;
pay a settlement of $67.5 million. It doesn’t look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:02.220
like they changed anything after this settlement&nbsp;
though as they just became even more aggressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:06.960
when it came to recruiting. The university&nbsp;
precisely priced their degrees so that the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:12.300
cost of the degree could be handled by government&nbsp;
grants and loans. This meant that basically anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:18.120
could afford to attend the school, but that was&nbsp;
kind of also the problem: basically anyone could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:23.280
afford to attend the school. This resulted in a&nbsp;
bunch of people who weren’t exactly academically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.960
inclined to join the school. These people&nbsp;
had very little chance of actually getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:32.700
through the coursework and obtaining the&nbsp;
degree meaning that much of the effort that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:37.620
these students spent at the institution would&nbsp;
be a complete waste. Yet, they were pushed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.880
recruiters to join anyway just so that the&nbsp;
institution could earn money from government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:48.540
grants. One group of people who have access to&nbsp;
bunch of government grants is veterans, and yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:54.000
the University of Phoenix would shamelessly target&nbsp;
veterans. They would allegedly setup recruiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:59.640
booths at military bases and promise the moon and&nbsp;
more to these veterans. At one point, the school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:05.700
would even get banned from recruiting at military&nbsp;
bases, but this wasn’t until after the damage had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:12.780
already been done. During the worst of it, only 5%&nbsp;
of students were actually getting a degree which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:19.860
means that 95% had paid for nothing or worse, they&nbsp;
were paying back loans for nothing. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:27.000
the FTC did step in and get the university to&nbsp;
partially refund 147,500 students and forgive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:34.920
some debt, but in the grand scheme of things this&nbsp;
was nothing. $191 million spread across 147,500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:40.140
student works out to a mere $1300 per student&nbsp;
on average which is nothing in comparison to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:45.720
the cost of tuition. So, it looks like the vast&nbsp;
majority of students will be left holding the bag.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:47.520
UNIVERSITY OF PHOENIX TODAY:
While the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:56.040
wasn’t able to do to a whole lot about the&nbsp;
situation, it looks like the media has. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.040 --> 00:11:01.200
more and more media outlets cover the institution&nbsp;
and all of their shady practices, it appears that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:06.660
less and less people end up joining. In fact,&nbsp;
enrollment has fallen off a cliff from nearly half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:12.600
a million in 2010 to less than a 100,000 in 2020.&nbsp;
This has forced the institution to scale back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:18.900
substanitally. In fact, the university expects&nbsp;
to only have one campus by 2025. They even had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.980
to give up their stadium as well which has since&nbsp;
been overtaken by State Farm. Looking forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:28.560
I suspect that this downtrend will only grow&nbsp;
stronger. Here’s the thing, even if all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.180
reasons outlined in this video doesn’t dissuade&nbsp;
people from joining, one factor definitely will:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:39.600
job prospects. Even if the professors, classes,&nbsp;
and courses are all stellar at the university,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:45.300
graduates would still have a tough time finding&nbsp;
a job simply due to the terrible reputation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:50.880
the university. If you have this institution on&nbsp;
your resume, employers will almost definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:55.200
question your judgement and wonder why&nbsp;
you joined a for profit instituion. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:59.880
if you’re looking to get a degree, this should&nbsp;
probably be one of the top institutions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:05.220
you avoid like the plague, but that’s just what&nbsp;
I think. Do you think that college in general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:10.380
is a scam? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if you hope that the students&nbsp;&nbsp;

